    <region left="20" top="68108" width="1560" height="27" color="#0f2a44" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: bold; font-style: normal; font-size: 12px;">17.1  Every verdict in one place</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="68143" width="1560" height="23" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">All of these must be greater than 1. Each line is the ratio itself, then what to change if it fails.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="68174" width="1560" height="51" color="#444444" fontSize="9" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
      <content>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">TWO OF THEM CANNOT BE READ THAT WAY. k.auxr asks whether the auxiliary winding, wired STRAIGHT to VCC, stays under the 25 V rating; here a Zener regulator sits in between and k.VCC asks the same question of its output - see section 14. k.lam is a NO-LOAD</p>
        <p style="font-weight: normal; font-style: normal; font-size: 9px;">gain condition, and the hardware limit it stands in for is k.ceil. Every other row is a real test, and the text beside it is what to change when it reads below 1.</p>
      </content>
    </text>
    </region>
    <region left="20" top="68395" width="1560" height="27" color="#0f2a44" fontSize="12" isBreakable="false">
    <text lang="eng" fontFamily="Arial" fontSize="10">
